            {
                "entry_id": "f30396b9-ae10-3ce1-a07f-3880434d4b7d",
                "word": "Ojibwe",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "39732171-e011-313f-8835-c2964156ca2c",
                        "entry_id": "f30396b9-ae10-3ce1-a07f-3880434d4b7d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The language spoken by the native Algonquin people of central Canada, one of a closely related group of languages and dialects of the Algonquian branch of the Algic language family."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8784d8a8-d1a5-4131-97c5-5be9f01cc3ec",
                                "sense_id": "39732171-e011-313f-8835-c2964156ca2c",
                                "text": "On a Tuesday in April 2017, Janet Macbeth composed an all-office email. “My story,” read the subject line in Nishnaabemwin, the Ojibwe language.\n“Hello everyone,” the email continued in Nishnaabemwin. “My name is Janet.”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "da1e4710-92b4-428e-94f1-47a4aa9266b7",
                                "sense_id": "39732171-e011-313f-8835-c2964156ca2c",
                                "text": "Endangered language communities would be thrilled with and proud of that kind of exposure for their language, like when Star Wars was dubbed into Navajo and Ojibwe."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ec307f95-fcad-4c28-8327-8142b253c3ec",
                        "source_entry_id": "f30396b9-ae10-3ce1-a07f-3880434d4b7d",
